---
layout: article
title: Password protect a site or function
description: Create an Appwrite Firewall password challenge that asks visitors for a password before a site or function responds.
difficulty: beginner
readtime: 6
---

A password challenge asks for a password before a request reaches your site or function. Visitors in a browser see a password page. Scripts, servers, and CI jobs send the password in a request header.

Use it when you need to keep a site or endpoint private without building sign-in into the app:

- Pre-launch and staging sites
- Previews you share with a client
- Internal tools and admin routes
- Internal function endpoints called by cron jobs or CI

A password challenge is a [challenge](/docs/products/firewall/actions#challenge) rule with **Challenge type** set to **Password**. It applies to **Sites** and **Functions** only. The default **Compute** type, a proof-of-work puzzle, is covered in [Challenge automated traffic](/docs/products/firewall/challenge-bots).

This guide protects the `/admin` path of a site.

# Create the password rule {% #create-the-password-rule %}

1. Open **Firewall** in your project.
2. Click **Create rule**.
3. Enter a **Rule name**, for example `Password protect admin`.
4. Set **Resource type** to **Sites** and select your site.
5. Under conditions, set **Path** **Starts with** `/admin`. To protect the whole site, add no conditions.
6. Under **Then**, choose **Challenge**.
7. Set **Challenge type** to **Password**.
8. Enter a **Password** of 8 to 256 bytes. Some characters, like emoji or non-Latin letters, take more than one byte.
9. Keep **Wrong guesses allowed** at `10` and **Counted over (seconds)** at `300`. See [Guess limits](#guess-limits).
10. Set **TTL (seconds)** to how long a visitor stays signed in after entering the password.
11. Click **Create rule**.

The rules list shows the rule's challenge type and guess limit, for example `Password · 10 guesses / 5 min`. **Difficulty** applies only to compute challenges, so it is hidden for password rules.

To ask for the password only at certain times, for example outside office hours, turn on **Schedule** on the rule.

# What visitors see {% #what-visitors-see %}

A browser that opens a protected page gets a password page. The right password sets a clearance cookie for that rule, and the visitor reaches the page without being asked again until the rule's **TTL** runs out. The TTL is at least `900` seconds (15 minutes).

A clearance only opens the rule that issued it. A visitor cleared on one password rule is still asked by every other password rule, even on the same site.

Requests that are not browser page loads, such as `fetch` calls and requests for images or scripts, get `403` instead of a password page when they carry no clearance.

# Send the password from code {% #send-the-password-from-code %}

Scripts, servers, cron jobs, and CI cannot fill in the password page. Send the password in the `X-Appwrite-WAF-Password` header instead:

```sh
SITE_URL="https://example.com"
PASSWORD="your-password"
curl "$SITE_URL/admin/report" \
  -H "X-Appwrite-WAF-Password: $PASSWORD"
```

- A correct password passes the request and also sets the clearance cookie.
- A wrong password gets `403` with no password page.
- Appwrite removes the header before the request reaches your site or function, so your code never sees the password.
- The header is checked only against the rule that matched the request.

Browsers should use the password page. A cross-origin `fetch` call that sets this header fails the CORS preflight.

# Create the rule through the API {% #create-the-rule-through-the-api %}

Create a password rule with `POST /v1/waf/rules/challenge` and `challengeType` set to `password`:

```sh
curl -X POST https://cloud.appwrite.io/v1/waf/rules/challenge \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>" \
  -H "X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>" \
  -d '{
    "ruleId": "unique()",
    "name": "Password protect admin",
    "resourceType": "sites",
    "resourceId": "<SITE_ID>",
    "priority": 100,
    "enabled": true,
    "challengeType": "password",
    "password": "<PASSWORD>",
    "attempts": 10,
    "attemptsInterval": 300,
    "ttl": 1800,
    "conditions": [
      { "method": "startsWith", "attribute": "path", "values": ["/admin"] }
    ]
  }'
```

| Field | Purpose | Limits |
|-------|---------|--------|
| `password` | The password visitors enter | `8` to `256` bytes. Required on create |
| `attempts` | Wrong guesses allowed per visitor network | `1` to `100`, default `10` |
| `attemptsInterval` | Seconds the wrong guesses are counted over | `60` to `86400`, default `300` |
| `ttl` | Seconds a visitor stays cleared after entering the password | `900` to `86400` |

Update the rule with `PATCH /v1/waf/rules/challenge/{ruleId}`. Leave `password` out to keep the stored password. Switching an existing compute rule to `password` requires a password.

The API rejects:

- `password`, `attempts`, or `attemptsInterval` on a `compute` challenge, with `waf_rule_invalid_config`.
- A password rule on the `api` resource, with `waf_rule_invalid_resource`.

The API counts the password length in bytes, so a multibyte character such as an emoji uses more than one of the 256.

# Guess limits {% #guess-limits %}

Each rule allows **Wrong guesses allowed** (`attempts`) wrong passwords per **Counted over** window (`attemptsInterval`). Guesses are counted per visitor network: per IP address for IPv4, and per `/64` for IPv6. Guesses through the password page and through the header share the same count.

Once a network goes over the limit, Appwrite answers `429` with a `Retry-After` header until the window ends.

# Change the password {% #change-the-password %}

1. Open **Firewall** and open the rule's actions menu.
2. Click **Update**.
3. Enter the new password. Leave the field blank to keep the current one.
4. Save the rule.

Changing the password ends every existing clearance for that rule immediately. Visitors and scripts need the new password on their next request.

Appwrite stores only a salted Argon2 hash of the password, the same way it stores account passwords. The API never returns the password, so keep a copy where your team can find it.

# Scope and limits {% #scope-and-limits %}

- **Sites and functions only.** A password rule does not protect Appwrite API endpoints. Executions created with `POST /v1/functions/{functionId}/executions` follow the function's execute permissions, not the password rule on its domain. To control access to files, use [permissions](/docs/products/storage/permissions) or [file tokens](/docs/products/storage/file-tokens).
- **Not per-user authentication.** Everyone with the password gets the same access, and you cannot revoke one person without changing the password for everyone. Use [Appwrite Auth](/docs/products/auth) when you need individual accounts.

